    "narrative": "For our August 29th slate, **Seiya Suzuki** (73.90) and **Brice Turang** (70.37) lead the charge as our strongest plays. **Seiya Suzuki** benefits from a massive 19% SP skill boost against Abbott, and his spot in the #2 hole ensures maximum RBI/Run opportunities. Meanwhile, **Brice Turang** is a priority start due to our emphasis on speed; the favorable matchup against Quantrill and a 100% stolen base probability index\u2014driven by the pitcher's slow delivery\u2014makes him our most valuable asset for category-scarcity management today. **Josh Naylor** also anchors our lineup at 66.82, providing stability in the heart of the order as he faces Soriano.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision involved **Griffin Conine**, who sits on the bench with a 43.60 score. While his platoon edge is notable, he failed to crack the starting nine because he lacks the elite sprint speed profile that we are currently prioritizing to maintain our edge in SB. Conversely, **Corbin Carroll** (57.48) and **Jose Caballero** (58.65) remain in the lineup despite lower raw scores because their wheels are essential for our daily efficiency goals. We are closely monitoring the risk profile of **Corbin Carroll** today; he faces a sub-3.00 xERA arm in Marte, which effectively stifled his scoring potential despite his prime position in the order.\n\nFinally, we must navigate the bottom of our roster carefully. **Gabriel Moreno** (62.88) gets a significant boost from Wilkinson\u2019s 6.01 xERA, providing a rare high-efficiency start for a backstop. However, keep an eye on **Mickey Moniak**, who sits at 54.94; while he benefits from the SP skill projection, his negative platoon split against P\u00e9rez makes him a candidate to be subbed out if our daily game caps start to tighten. With **James Wood**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, and **Vinnie Pasquantino** confirmed out, we are relying on our depth players like **Henry Bolte** to maintain our momentum in the Roto standings.",
